Here is a simple Java implementation of the requirement. This program accepts two numbers from user to define a range and uses a while loop to print prime numbers in a decreasing order. Error handling is implemented in case the user provides invalid inputs.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

    int lowerLimit, upperLimit;

    try {
      System.out.print("Enter the lower limit: ");
      lowerLimit = scanner.nextInt();

      System.out.print("Enter the upper limit: ");
      upperLimit = scanner.nextInt();
    } catch (Exception e) {
      System.out.println("Invalid Input. Please enter a valid integer.");
      return;
    }

    System.out.println("Prime numbers between " + lowerLimit + " and " + upperLimit + " are:");

    while (upperLimit >= lowerLimit) {
      if (isPrime(upperLimit)) {
        System.out.println(upperLimit);
      }
      upperLimit--;
    }
  }

  static boolean isPrime(int num) {
    if (num <= 1) {
      return false;
    }
    for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
      if (num % i == 0) {
        return false;
      }
    }
    return true;
  }
}
```
Here, Scanner is used to get the input from user. The "try-catch" block is used to handle invalid inputs (non-numerical). A while loop is used to iterate from the upperLimit to the lowerLimit in decreasing order. In each iteration, the isPrime() method is called to determine if the current number is prime or not. If it is, then it's printed. If not, the iteration proceeds to the next lower number. The isPrime() method checks each number if it's divisible by any number up to its square root. If it is, then it's not a prime number. If it's only divisible by 1 and itself, then it's a prime number.

