Here is a basic implementation of the TriStateToggleSwitchComponent. We're utilizing the useState hook from React to manage the state of the toggle.

```JavaScript
import React, {useState} from 'react';

const TriStateToggleSwitchComponent = (props) => {
  const [state, setState] = useState('undefined');

  const clickHandler = () => {
    setState((prevState) => {
      switch(prevState) {
        case 'undefined': return 'on';
        case 'on': return 'off';
        case 'off': return 'undefined';
        default: return 'undefined';
      }
    });
  };

  const getStyle = () => {
    switch(state) {
      case 'undefined': return props.undefinedStyle;
      case 'on': return props.onStyle;
      case 'off': return props.offStyle;
      default: return {};
    }
  }

  return (
    <div onClick={clickHandler} style={getStyle()}>{state}</div>
  );
};

export default TriStateToggleSwitchComponent;
```

This component starts in the 'undefined' state. Each click on the div tag where the toggle is held will change its state. It will proceed from 'undefined' to 'on', from 'on' to 'off', and from 'off' back to 'undefined'.

The getStyle function corresponds the currently active state to the provided styles. If no styles are provided, it will default to none. However, each click will not only change the displayed text ('undefined', 'on', 'off') but also switch the div's style attributes according to the prop styles passed. 

To use the component, do it like in the given example in your desired render function. Simply replace the style objects with the desired styles for each state. Of course, you are not limited to background color. You can add other styles as well: 

```JavaScript
<div>
  <TriStateToggleSwitchComponent 
    onStyle={{backgroundColor: 'green'}} 
    offStyle={{backgroundColor: 'red'}} 
    undefinedStyle={{backgroundColor: 'grey'}}/>
</div>
```

