The appropriate data structure for this kind of problem would be a Set or a Hash set in case if value retrieval needs to be quick. Sets in most programming languages can contain unique elements only.

The specific style of organization would, of course, depend on the full requirements, including speed and memory usage considerations, the need for persistence, and how data will be accessed and used.

Enumerable in Ruby, Set in Python, and HashSet in Java are representations of this abstract data structure in different programming languages. In case you want to also retain the order of insertion, you might consider using LinkedHashSet in Java. 

Here's a simple Java illustration of using HashSet for this task:

```java
import java.util.HashSet;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        HashSet<String> ids = new HashSet<>();

        ids.add("id1"); // Add initial IDs
        ids.add("id2");

        // Adding duplicate ID
        if(!ids.add("id1")) {
            System.out.println("Duplicate ID found: id1");
        }

        // Print all IDs
        for(String id : ids) {
            System.out.println(id);
        }
    }
}
```

In the example above, we're storing unique IDs in a HashSet. If we try to add a duplicate ID, the `add()` method will return false, letting us know that the ID already exists in the set. Meanwhile, if a new user ID is added, it will be accepted into the data structure without an issue.

