Since Vue.js doesn't provide a built-in approach to create a pie chart, most developers use a third-party library to achieve this functionality. One of those libraries is Chart.js which makes it simple to create various types of charts.

First, we need to install vue-chartjs and Chart.js libraries to our project. We can add these to our package.json using npm or yarn:

npm:
```bash
npm install vue-chartjs chart.js --save
```

yarn:
```bash
yarn add vue-chartjs chart.js
```

Now that we have vue-chartjs and Chart.js installed, let's implement our component:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <canvas id="myChart"></canvas>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import { Pie } from 'vue-chartjs'

export default {
  extends: Pie,
  data() {
    return {
      datacollection: {
        labels: [],
        datasets: [{
          data: [],
          backgroundColor: [],   // add colors if you want
        }],
      },
      options: {
        responsive: true,
        maintainAspectRatio: false
      }
    }
  },
  async mounted() {
    // Here you can put your API call to fill the values
    // We use hardcoded values for this example
    const data = [
      {category: 'Electronics', percentage: 40, color: '#ff6384'},
      {category: 'Clothing', percentage: 30, color: '#36a2eb'},
      {category: 'Home & Garden', percentage: 15, color: '#cc65fe'},
      {category: 'Sports & Outdoors', percentage: 15, color: '#ffce56'}
    ]
    data.forEach(item => {
      this.datacollection.labels.push(item.category)
      this.datacollection.datasets[0].data.push(item.percentage)
      this.datacollection.datasets[0].backgroundColor.push(item.color)
    })
    this.renderChart(this.datacollection, this.options)
  }
}
</script>
```

In this code, we have a Vue component that, in it's mounted() function, populates the graph with pie slices for each item in the data array. The pie slices are colored differently and their sizes are based on the percentage values of each item. The labels property of datacollection is an array of strings which will be the labels for the pie slices. 

We are overriding the mounted() lifecycle event of Vue.js component to fetch the data and populate our graph. Calling this.renderChart() with the prepared datacollection will then render our pie chart. 

This only involves static data but in a real-world scenario, you could easily replace it with asynchronous API calls. The same process may be followed (fetch data, populate the labels and data arrays, and finally call this.renderChart()). Remember to make sure to handle loading state and exceptions that can be thrown by APIs as well.

