const maxElement = (l) => {
   let max = l[-1];
   for(let i = 0; i < l.length; i++) {
      if(l[i] > max) {
         max = l[i];
     } 
   }
   return max;
}

The code you provided has a minor mistake. In JavaScript, l[-1] doesn't retrieve the last element from the array. It's NaN when it tries to compare the first elements in line (l[i] > max).

Let's rectify it by setting max to the first element of the list (i.e., l[0]). Here is the rectified code:

```javascript
const maxElement = (l) => {
    let max = l[0];  // Setting max as the first element.
    for(let i = 0; i < l.length; i++) {
        if(l[i] > max) {
            max = l[i];
        } 
    }
    return max;
}
```
Now, each time through the loop, if l[i] > max, then max is updated to become l[i]. The loop will iterate through every element once, and by the end, max will hold the maximum value.

For time complexity: 
This function has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of elements in the input array. This is because the function iterates over each element of the array exactly once.

For space complexity: 
The space complexity of the function is O(1), because the amount of extra space required by the function does not increase with the size of the input array. The function uses a single variable 'max' to keep track of the maximum element, so the space usage is constant.

