Here is a simple C# program to find out if two strings are anagrams of each other or not.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        try
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Enter first string:");
            string str1 = Console.ReadLine();
            Console.WriteLine("Enter second string:");
            string str2 = Console.ReadLine();

            if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(str1) || string.IsNullOrEmpty(str2) || 
                !Regex.IsMatch(str1, @"^[a-zA-Z]+$") || !Regex.IsMatch(str2, @"^[a-zA-Z]+$"))
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Error: Both inputs must be non-empty strings and contain only alphabetic characters.");
                return;
            }

            bool areAnagrams = AreAnagrams(str1, str2, out int uniqueCharacters);

            if (areAnagrams)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("true");
                Console.WriteLine($"Number of unique characters: {uniqueCharacters}");
            }
            else
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Error: The two strings are not anagrams of each other.");
            }
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Error: " + e.Message);
        }
    }

    static bool AreAnagrams(string str1, string str2, out int uniqueCharacters)
    {
        str1 = str1.ToLower();
        str2 = str2.ToLower();
        string combined = str1 + str2;

        uniqueCharacters = combined.Distinct().Count();

        var charCount1 = str1.GroupBy(c => c).ToDictionary(g => g.Key, g => g.Count());
        var charCount2 = str2.GroupBy(c => c).ToDictionary(g => g.Key, g => g.Count());

        return charCount1.Keys.Count == charCount2.Keys.Count && charCount1.All(x => charCount2.ContainsKey(x.Key) && charCount2[x.Key] == x.Value);
    }
}
```

The "AreAnagrams" method, which is being called from the "Main" method, returns a boolean indicating whether the two input strings are anagrams or not. It also outputs the number of unique characters across both strings. 

It first changes both strings to lower case and combines them to get the count of unique characters. Then, it groups and counts the individual characters in each string and checks if both strings have the same set and the same count of characters. If they do, the strings are anagrams. 

The time complexity of the implemented algorithm is O(n), as the main complexity comes from checking the frequency of characters on both strings. The space complexity is also O(n), as we're storing these frequencies in hash tables. 

The "Main" method first verifies that both strings are non-empty and contain only alphabetic characters. If any of these checks fail, a meaningful error message is printed.

If you want to check for anagrams in a case-sensitive way, you can remove the `str1 = str1.ToLower();` and `str2 = str2.ToLower();` lines.

