Selectors, filters, and pseudo-classes in CSS are used to target specific HTML elements based on their attributes, state, and position in the document tree to apply styles selectively.

Attribute selectors:
Attribute selectors target elements based on their attribute values. The syntax is as follows: element[attribute=value].

Example:
```css
/* Select all input elements with 'type' attribute set to 'text' */
input[type="text"] {
  background-color: lightgrey;
}
```

Child and sibling selectors:
Child and sibling selectors target elements based on their relationship with other elements in the document tree.

Example:
```css
/* Select all direct child <p> elements of a <div>*/
div > p {
  color: red;
}

/* Select any <p> element that comes immediately after another <p> */
p + p {
  text-indent: 2em;
}

/* Select every <p> element that is preceded by another <p> */
p ~ p {
  text-indent: 2em;
}
```

Pseudo-classes:
Pseudo-classes are added to selectors to target elements in a specific state, e.g., their state when being hovered over or activated.

Example:
```css
/* Select all links in their normal state */
a:link {
  color: blue;
}

/* Select all links when being hovered over */
a:hover {
  color: red;
}

/* Select all links when being clicked or activated */
a:active {
  color: green;
}

/* Select all even rows in a table */
tr:nth-child(even) {
  background-color: lightgrey;
}

/* Select all odd rows in a table */
tr:nth-child(odd) {
  background-color: white;
}
```

In summary, CSS selectors, filters, and pseudo-classes can be employed to target and style HTML elements based on their attributes, state, and position in the document tree. By combining these techniques, you can achieve efficient and precise control over the styling of the elements in your HTML documents.

