To create a React Native application that displays the current date and time, we first need to set up the necessary state and timer in our component. We can make use of the useState and useEffect hooks.

Here is what the App could look like:

```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { StyleSheet, Text, View } from 'react-native';

export default function App() {
  const [date, setDate] = useState(new Date());

  // Update the date state every 5 seconds
  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setInterval(() => setDate(new Date()), 5000);
    // Clear the interval on unmount
    return () => clearInterval(timer);
  }, []);

  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Text style={styles.text}>
        Current Date and Time - {date.toLocaleString()}
      </Text>
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    backgroundColor: '#fff',
    alignItems: 'center',
    justifyContent: 'center',
  },
  text: {
    fontSize: 20,
  },
});
```

Please note that you should always clear your timers when you no longer need them. This is done by returning a function from the useEffect hook that calls clearInterval with the timer id.

This will automatically clear the interval whenever the component is unmounted, which happens when your app is closed, or when the user navigates to a different screen. This is important to prevent potential memory leaks in your app.

One more thing to note, `toLocaleString()` method is used to convert date object to a string, and it will show the date and time based on the user's locale settings. For a uniform representation, you can prefer to format the date string as per your requirement using either built-in JavaScript date functions or can use libraries like moment.js for advanced need.

