This Python function `last_occurrence_position` receives two parameters: a list of integers `lst` and an integer `target`. It initializes a variable `position` to `-1` indicating that the target integer has not been found. Then it loops over the list from start to end (as indicated by `range(len(lst))`). Inside the loop, if it finds the target integer (`if lst[i] == target:`), it updates the `position` to the current index. Note that if the target integer occurs multiple times in the list, `position` will always contain the latest index due to the traversal direction. After the loop ends, the function returns the `position`. If the target integer does not exist in the list, the function will return `-1`.

