The problem mentions two main points that should be considered in the solution - calculating the mean value of the numbers and handling possible integer overflows. 

The mean value of a set of numbers is calculated by adding them all and dividing the sum by the count of numbers. Modern C++ has a function std::accumulate which can be used for summing all elements. 

To handle integer overflow, we can opt for a lazy way of dividing first and then sum. This will work fine assuming that our numbers are all positive which is the case for this problem.

Here is my solution:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <numeric>
using namespace std;

double MeanValue(const vector<int>& arr) {
    // calculate sum of integers
    double sum = 0.0;
    for (const auto& num : arr) {
        sum += static_cast<double>(num) / arr.size();
    }

    return sum;
}

int main() {
    vector<int> arr = {3, 5, 8, 10, 14, 16, 22, 27, 33, 44};
    cout << "Mean Value = " << MeanValue(arr) << endl;

    return 0;
}
```
This program calculates the mean value for the array of integers and prints it. It prevents an integer overflow by dividing every integer with the count of integers before adding to the sum.

