```javascript
function primeAnalysis(n) {
    if (n <= 1) {
        return `${n} is not prime because prime numbers are positive integers greater than 1.`;
    } else if (n <= 3) {
        return `${n} is a prime number.`;
    } else if (n % 2 === 0 || n % 3 === 0) {
        return `${n} is not prime because it's divisible by 2 or 3.`;
    }

    let i = 5;

    while (i * i <= n) {
        if (n % i === 0 || n % (i + 2) === 0) {
            return `${n} is not prime because it's divisible by ${i} or ${i + 2}.`;
        }

        i += 6;
    }

    return `${n} is a prime number.`;
}
```
This function will work for any given positive integer `n`. It works by checking the divisibility of `n` starting from 5 to the square root of `n`, because a larger factor of the number must be a multiple of smaller factor that has already been checked, it increments the step by 6, as at least one number among N, N+2 and N+4 is divisible by 3, (because every third number is divisible by three), and so it only checks the numbers N and N+2 and skips N+4.
