class PrintPhrase {
    synchronized void print() {
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " is printing a phrase.");

        int sleepTime = (int)(Math.random() * 2000);
        try {
            Thread.sleep(sleepTime);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```
In this program, there is a `PrintPhrase` class with a synchronized `print` method. This method will print a phrase and then sleep for a random amount of time between 0 and 2 seconds. Because the `print` method is synchronized, only one thread can access it at a time. 

In the `main` method, 3 threads are created which each call the `print` method 5 times. When all the threads are started, they will each attempt to call the `print` method, but due to the synchronization, only one will be able to do so at a time. As a result, each line of output will be printed one at a time by each thread.

