There are various methods to achieve vertical alignment in CSS. Here is a solution using CSS flexbox.

```css
.wrapper {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    height: 100vh;
}
```

Explanation: By setting the `display` property of the `.wrapper` to `flex`, it will become a flex container and its children will become flex items. 

The `align-items: center;` line aligns the flex items along the cross axis i.e. vertically center aligns the items.

The `justify-content: center;` line aligns the flex items along the main axis i.e. horizontally center aligns the items.

`height: 100vh;` sets the height of the `.wrapper` element to 100% of the viewport's height, guaranteeing that it will occupy the full height of the screen. This is important for proper vertical alignment as if the `height` of the `.wrapper` isn't explicitly set (or isn't derived from its content) then the vertical alignment would only be within the actual height of the `.wrapper` which won't necessarily be the full height of the viewport.

Note that not all ways to vertically align using CSS entail the same level of browser compatibility. Therefore, it is always a good practice to check the compatibility of the CSS property with different browsers before you decide to use it.

