```java
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean;

public class InputProcessor {

    private static final Object lock = new Object();
    private static boolean dataAvailable = false;
    private static String data = null;

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread inputThread = new Thread(() -> {
            Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

            while (true) {
                synchronized (lock) {
                    while (dataAvailable) {
                        try {
                            lock.wait();
                        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                            e.printStackTrace();
                        }
                    }

                    System.out.println("Waiting for input: ");
                    data = scanner.nextLine();
                    dataAvailable = true;
                    lock.notifyAll();
                }
            }
        });

        Thread processingThread = new Thread(() -> {
            while (true) {
                synchronized (lock) {
                    while (!dataAvailable) {
                        try {
                            lock.wait();
                        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                            e.printStackTrace();
                        }
                    }

                    System.out.println("Processing: " + data);
                    dataAvailable = false;
                    lock.notifyAll();
                }
            }
        });

        inputThread.start();
        processingThread.start();
    }
}
```

This program has two threads, one for taking the input (`inputThread`) and one for processing the input (`processingThread`). `dataAvailable` is a shared flag that indicates whether there's input available to be processed or not. The flag is used for coordination and synchronization, to ensure that the `processingThread` only consumes the input when `dataAvailable` is `true` and `inputThread` only reads new input when `dataAvailable` is `false`.

Potential erroneous code:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {

    String data = null;

    Thread inputThread = new Thread(() -> {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        while (true) {
            System.out.println("Waiting for input: ");
            data = scanner.nextLine();
        }
    });

    Thread processingThread = new Thread(() -> {
        while (true) {
            if (data != null) {
                System.out.println("Processing: " + data);
                data = null;
            }
        }
    });

    inputThread.start();
    processingThread.start();
}
```

There are several problems with the above code:

1. There's no synchronization among threads. Even though `data` is shared between two threads, there's no mechanism to ensure that there are not any race conditions where input could be processed before it's completely read or new input could overwrite the old one before it's processed.

2. The processing thread continuously checks for new input, even when there's not any available. This is known as "busy waiting" and it can consume a lot of CPU resources unnecessarily.

3. The input may not be visible immediately to the processing thread after being set in the input thread due to inconsistencies in cached data across different CPU cores. This is a common problem in multi-threaded programming known as "cache coherence". Java's `volatile` keyword or `AtomicReference` can be used to solve this problem.

