```
public class HelloWorld {

    public static void main(String[] args) { 

        String str = "World"; 
        char[] charArray = str.toCharArray(); 
        char charPos; 
        charPos = charArray[1];
        System.out.println( charPos); 

        charPos = charArray[5]; 
        System.out.println(charPos); 

        str = "";
        charArray = str.toCharArray();
        charPos = charArray[0];
        System.out.println( charPos);
    } 
}
```

The Java code prints the character at different positions in the given string. It first assigns the string "World" to the variable 'str', converts it to a character array 'charArray', and then prints the second character (Java is 0-indexed, so index 1 equals the second letter, which is 'o' in this case). However, it will then attempt to access the sixth character and print it. Since the string "World" has only five characters, it does not have a sixth character. This will cause an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.

Moreover, the code will again assign an empty string to the variable 'str'. It then attempts to print the first character of this empty string. It will again lead to an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException since the array 'charArray' has no elements now.

To address these issues, it would be wise to check the length of the string or the array before attempting to access a particular character from it. This could be done with an if statement similar to this:

```
if (str.length() > position) {
