Here is a solution in Java. We will make use of the formula that the sum of n squares is n*(n+1)*(2n+1)/6. We can calculate the sum for all numbers less than 'k' and 'n' and subtract the smaller from the larger.

Here is a Java-based class for this job:

```java
public class QuadraticSum {

    private static final int MOD = 1_000_000_007;

    // Function to calculate the sum of squares
    private static long sumOfSquares(long n) {
        return ((n * (n + 1) % MOD) * ((2 * n + 1) % MOD) / 6) % MOD;
    }

    // Function to calculate the sum of squares from k to n
    public static long sumOfSquares(long k, long n) {
        if (k > n)
            return 0;

        long upper = sumOfSquares(n);
        long lower = sumOfSquares(k - 1);

        return (upper - lower + MOD) % MOD;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(sumOfSquares(5, 10)); // Output: 330
    }
}
```
This code provides an `O(1)` time complexity for the sum of squares function, and the overall complexity is also `O(1)` because it performs a constant amount of operations. This is much better than an `O(n)` solution where usually you would use a loop to iterate from `k` to `n`.

The space complexity is also `O(1)` because it only uses a constant amount of space to store the variables and doesn't use any additional data structures that would grow with input size.

