Let's take an example of a class named "Student". First, we can define the class and its attributes - name, id, and grades. Then we can create a constructor to initialize these attributes when creating an object of the class and destructor.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Student {
private: 
    string mName;
    int mId;
    float mGrades;
public: 
    //default constructor
    Student()
    {
        mName = "Not Set";
        mId = 0;
        mGrades = 0;
    }

    // constructor with parameters (overloading)
    Student(string name, int id, float grades)
    : mName(name), mId(id), mGrades(grades)
    {
    }

    //copy constructor (deep copy)
    Student(const Student& other)
    : mName(other.mName), mId(other.mId), mGrades(other.mGrades)
    {
    }

    //destructor
    ~Student()
    {
        cout<<"Destructor called for " << mName << endl;
    }

    //accessors
    string getName() const { return mName; }
    int getId() const { return mId; }
    float getGrades() const { return mGrades; }

    //mutators
    void setName(string val) { mName = val; }
    void setId(int val) { mId = val; }
    void setGrades(float val) { mGrades = val; }
};
```

In this example "Student" class demonstrates encapsulation by hiding its internal details (attributes) using the “private” keyword and exposes methods to interact with them in controlled and safe fashion.

Student's constructor method is overloaded to accept zero or three arguments and performs a deep copy when an existing instance is provided. A new instance of Student is entirely independent of the copied instance i.e., modifications to the new instance won't affect the original one. This is opposite to shallow copy where copied instance's fields point to same location in memory as original's.

Returning objects from functions is also handled properly by compiler. It either uses move semantics (if available, it's an optimization brought by C++11) or copy constructor to create a copy of object to be returned. It won't cause problems unless your code explicitly prohibits copying or moving.

In object-oriented programming, polymorphism is a concept where a name can be used for different types. In order to implement polymorphism, we need base and derived classes. Here's an example:

```cpp
class Person {
public:
    virtual void display() {
        cout << "This is a person." << endl;
    }
};

class Student : public Person {
public:
    void display() override {
        cout << "This is a student." << endl;
    }
};
```

In the above example, `display()` in `Person` is a virtual function, and `display()` in `Student` overrides it. When a `display()` function call is made on a `Person` pointer, the corresponding `display()` function is called, which is determined by the actual type of object at run time. This is polymorphism.

