Here is a simple implementation of a multithreaded Java program to calculate the Fibonacci sequence. This program creates a new thread for each calculation. A Vector is used to hold the results, due to its synchronized nature.

Before running this code, note that Fibonacci calculations are exponential in nature, and calculating high numbers can take a lot of time and computing resources.

```java
import java.util.Vector;

public class MultiThreadedFibonacci {
    private static Vector<Long> fib = new Vector<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Initialize the first two values
        fib.add(0L);
        fib.add(1L);

        // Start calculation
        new FibonacciCalculator(40).start();
    }

    // FibonacciCalculator is a Thread that calculates a Fibonacci number
    private static class FibonacciCalculator extends Thread {
        private int n;

        FibonacciCalculator(int n) {
            this.n = n;
        }

        @Override
        public void run() {
            if (n < 0) return;
            if (fib.size() > n) {
                printFibonacci();
            } else {
                // Create threads for the previous two numbers
                FibonacciCalculator thread1 = new FibonacciCalculator(n - 1);
                FibonacciCalculator thread2 = new FibonacciCalculator(n - 2);
                thread1.start();
                thread2.start();
                try {
                    // Wait for the two threads to finish
                    thread1.join();
                    thread2.join();
                    // Calculate the nth Fibonacci number
                    synchronized (fib) {
                        fib.add(n, fib.get(n - 1) + fib.get(n - 2));
                    }
                    printFibonacci();
                } catch (InterruptedException ignored) {
                }
            }
        }

        private void printFibonacci() {
            System.out.println(n + ": " + fib.get(n));
        }
    }
}
```

This program starts from calculating the 40th Fibonacci number, and for each number it spawns two new threads to calculate the two previous numbers. Those threads will in turn spawn more threads, until we reach the base cases which were initially added to the Vector. After calculating a number, it's printed to the console.

Synchronization is required when we add a new number to the Vector, as multiple threads can simultaneously try to add a number to the same index. The result would be incorrect if we didn't use synchronization.

