are said to be anagrams if the characters in one string can be rearranged to form the other string.

Here is the algorithm:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {

  public static boolean areAnagrams(String str1, String str2) {

        // If string lengths are not same, the strings are not anagrams
        if (str1.length() != str2.length()) {
            return false;
        }

        // Convert the strings to character arrays
        char[] str1Array = str1.toCharArray();
        char[] str2Array = str2.toCharArray();

        // Sort the character arrays
        Arrays.sort(str1Array);
        Arrays.sort(str2Array);

        // Compare sorted character arrays
        return Arrays.equals(str1Array, str2Array);
    }

  public static void main(String args[]) {

    String str1 = "listen";
    String str2 = "silent";
    System.out.println("Are \""+ str1 + "\" and \""+ str2 +"\" anagrams? " + areAnagrams(str1,str2));
  }
}
```

In the above program, first we convert the input strings to character arrays and then sort them using the `Arrays.sort()` method. Two strings are anagrams if and only if their sorted character arrays are equal. This condition is verified using the `Arrays.equals()` method. If the sorted arrays are equal, then the method returns true, else it returns false.

This solution has a time complexity of O(nlogn) due to the sorting operation. 

Note: This algorithm would consider "Cat" and "act" as not anagrams because it is case sensitive. If you want to consider them as anagrams you could add toLowerCase() when comparing the strings.

