Here's a refined version of the function "FileNameCheck". It incorporates regular expressions (through the "regexp" package) to verify the credibility of the file's designation according to your constraints.

```go
package main

import (
	"regexp"
	"strings"
)

// FileNameCheck returns a string ('Yes' or 'No') indicating if the inputted file name is valid or not.
// The file name is valid if:
// - It contains no more than three digits ('0'-'9').
// - It contains one and only one period '.'.
// - The substring before the period is not empty and begins with an alphabet character ('a'-'z' or 'A'-'Z').
// - The substring after the period is one of the following: ['txt', 'exe', 'dll'].
func FileNameCheck(file_designation string) string {
	// Check if the file's title (excluding the extension) has more than 3 digits
	digitCheck := regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z]\D*\d{0,3}\D*$`)
	if !digitCheck.MatchString(strings.Split(file_designation, ".")[0]) {
		return "No"
	}

	// Check if the substring before the period starts with an alphabet character and is not empty
	alphaCheck := regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z]\D*\.\D*$`)
	if !alphaCheck.MatchString(file_designation) {
		return "No"
	}

	// Check if the substring after the period is one of the following: ['txt', 'exe', 'dll']
	extensionCheck := regexp.MustCompile(`\.(txt|exe|dll)$`)
	if !extensionCheck.MatchString(file_designation) {
		return "No"
	}

	// If all checks passed, the file name is valid
	return "Yes"
}
```
Please note that the above code only checks for the rules that you have mentioned. Some operating systems may have other restrictions on file names. Also, this function does not check if the file actually exists.
