Before coding, let us understand what Java Array and ArrayList are:

Array: It is a fixed-length data structure that allows elements of the same data type. The length of an array cannot be changed once it is created.

ArrayList: ArrayList belongs to the Collection framework. It can dynamically grow and shrink as required. Unlike arrays, ArrayList can hold data of multiple data types.

Here's a sample code that demonstrates the usage of Array and ArrayList:

```Java
// Import ArrayList from java.util
import java.util.ArrayList;
//...

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Array declaration and initialization
        int[] arr = new int[5];
        arr[0] = 23;
        arr[1] = 45;
        arr[2] = 33;
        arr[3] = 70;
        arr[4] = 15;

        //Printing array elements
        for(int i=0; i<arr.length; i++){
            System.out.println("Array element "+i+": "+arr[i]);
        }

        // ArrayList declaration
        ArrayList<Integer> arrList = new ArrayList<Integer>();

        // Adding elements
        arrList.add(23);
        arrList.add(45);
        arrList.add(33);

        // Printing ArrayList elements
        for (int i = 0; i < arrList.size(); i++) {
            System.out.println("ArrayList element "+i+": "+arrList.get(i));
        }

        // Removing element
        arrList.remove(2); 
    }
}
```

Pros of Array:
1. It is not boxed.
2. There is no overhead of methods such as add(), get(), etc.

Cons of Array:
1. It cannot grow or shrink dynamically.
2. It can only have data of the same type.

Pros of ArrayList:
1. It can grow or shrink dynamically.
2. It can contain multiple data types.

Cons of ArrayList:
1. It requires boxing and unboxing.
2. It has overhead of methods like add(), remove(), get(), etc.

Regarding type safety, ArrayList in newer versions of Java can ensure type safety if you use Generics. An Array can contain only a single type of data so type safety is inherent.

As for capacity, the ArrayList can dynamically grow so it only needs to have its capacity adjusted if the estimated number of elements is known beforehand, and their number is large. On the other hand, an Array has a fixed capacity that needs to be defined upon its creation. Therefore, if the number of elements is not known, an ArrayList could be a better choice.

