Given the following array in JavaScript, write a function that finds the second largest number without using any in-built JavaScript methods. 
You should also consider edge cases, such as an array where all numbers are the same, or an array with only one or two elements.
Enhance the complexity by considering the time complexity of your function.

Here is a problematic example to get you started. Can you spot where the logic falls short? 

```javascript
let arr = [5, 19, 2, 46, 15, 31]

function secondLargest(arr) {
    let max = arr[0];
    let secondmax = arr[0];
    for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > max) {
            secondmax = max;
            max = arr[i];
        }
    }
    return secondmax;
}

console.log(secondLargest(arr)); 
```

>Note: This code fails to find the second maximum in the case where all elements are the same.

In the provided example, the error occurs because both max and secondmax are initialised with the first element of the array. In cases where all numbers are the same or there are only one or two elements in the array, the second maximum could not be found correctly due to this setting. 

If all elements are the same, the second maximum is the same as the maximum, but if there is only one element, the second maximum would not exist. 

The solution would be to set the initial values of max and secondmax as -Infinity which denotes the lowest possible number in JavaScript. Additionally, through the process of iteration, if no number is found to be greater than secondmax, then we should return undefined, which signifies that there is no second maximum.

Here's the corrected code:

```javascript
let arr = [5, 19, 2, 46, 15, 31];

function secondLargest(arr) {
    let max = -Infinity;
    let secondmax = -Infinity;
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > max) {
            secondmax = max;
            max = arr[i];
        }
        else if (arr[i] > secondmax && arr[i] < max) {
            secondmax = arr[i];
        }
    }
    return secondmax === -Infinity ? undefined : secondmax;
}

console.log(secondLargest(arr));
```

This function iterates through the given array only once, thus, its time complexity is O(n), where n is the number of elements in the array.

