Node.js uses NPM(Node Package Manager) to manage and share packages. To fetch a specific package, you need to create a script to check if the package is already installed. If it is installed, it needs to verify it's the latest version. If it isn't the latest, it should update it.

Here are the steps in node.js script:

1. Use the shelljs package for the system commands
2. Run npm list packageName to check if a package is installed
3. If installed, run npm outdated packageName to check the current version against the latest
4. If the packages aren't up-to-date, npm update packageName
5. Lastly, handle error cases

Here is the step-by-step implementation:

First, install shelljs via npm:
```sh
npm install shelljs
```

Then, in your script (let's name it checkPackage.js):

```js
var shell = require('shelljs');

const packageName = 'ejs';

// Check if the package is installed
if (!shell.which(packageName)) {
   console.log(`The package ${packageName} is not installed.`);
   shell.exec(`npm install ${packageName}`, function(code, stdout, stderr) {
      console.log('Attempting to pull the package from NPM');
      if(code != 0) {
         console.log(`Failed to install ${packageName}`)
         console.log('Error: ' + stderr);
      } else {
         console.log(`${packageName} is installed successfully.`);
      }
   });
} else {
   console.log(`The package ${packageName} is installed.`);
   // Check if the installed package is up to date
   shell.exec(`npm outdated ${packageName}`, function(code, stdout, stderr) {
       if (!stdout.includes('Current:')) {
          console.log(`${packageName} is up to date.`);
       } else {
         console.log(`Updating ${packageName}`);
         shell.exec(`npm update ${packageName}`, function(code, stdout, stderr) {
            if(code != 0) {
               console.log(`Failed to update ${packageName}`);
               console.log('Error: ' + stderr);
            } else {
               console.log(`${packageName} is updated successfully.`);
            }
         });
       }
   });
}
```

In the script, we first check if the given package is installed. We then check the installed package's version if it is installed, and, if the package is outdated, we update it.

This script catches the errors in commands' execution such as when the package doesn't exist in the ecosystem or the system loses connectivity while updating. However, this is a pretty naive solution and assumes that npm is correctly installed and available globally in the system. For any serious production scenario, you would want to use robust npm dependency managers and version lock files like yarn with yarn.lock or npm itself with package-lock.json files. These provide better control, fail-fast principle adherence, and repeatability.

Here is a JavaScript function that can be used to access and return elements from nested arrays. We use a for loop to iterate over each index in the input indices array, accessing nested elements one by one. We also perform some basic error checking to ensure the indices array is not empty, and that every index is within the bounds of the current array. If the given array or index is invalid, we throw an error.


```javascript
function accessNestedArray(arr, indices) {
    // Safety Check for no array or indices
    if (!arr || !indices || !indices.length) {
      throw new Error('Invalid Array or Indices');
    }

    let element = arr;

    for (let i = 0; i < indices.length; i++) {
        if (!Array.isArray(element) || indices[i] >= element.length) {
          // The index doesn't exist, so throw an error
          throw new Error(`Array or Index at nesting level ${i} does not exist`);
        }

        // Access the next level of the array
        element = element[indices[i]];
    }
    return element;
} 
```

