Here is an example of how you could solve this problem using a functional component in React. Below, we are creating a component using Hooks. We also use the useState Hook to create a state variable that will be used to keep track of the data entered into the superior form module.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from "react";

const MyComponent = () => {
  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({ fieldOne: "", fieldTwo: "" });

  // Handler to change data in both forms
  const handleChange = (e) => {
    const { name, value } = e.target;

    // Update form data
    setFormData({
      ...formData,
      [name]: value
    });
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Superior Form Module</h2>
      <form>
        <label>
          Field One:
          <input
            type="text"
            name="fieldOne"
            value={formData.fieldOne}
            onChange={handleChange}
          />
        </label>

        <label>
          Field Two:
          <input
            type="text"
            name="fieldTwo"
            value={formData.fieldTwo}
            onChange={handleChange}
          />
        </label>
      </form>

      <h2>Subordinate Form Module</h2>
      <form>
        <label>
          Field One:
          <input
            type="text"
            readOnly
            name="fieldOne"
            value={formData.fieldOne}
          />
        </label>

        <label>
          Field Two:
          <input
            type="text"
            readOnly
            name="fieldTwo"
            value={formData.fieldTwo}
          />
        </label>
      </form>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;
```

In this component, we have two forms, a superior form module and a subordinate form module. With the `handleChange` function, when users type into the superior form module, it updates the state variable 'formData' and this in turn is used to update the values in both the superior and subordinate form module.

It's important to note that the readOnly attribute is added to the input fields in the subordinate form module to prevent users from editing the data in that module directly. The data in the subordinate form module can only be updated by making changes in the superior form module.

