In the above code, the 'if' statement contains a semantic error where we assigned 'b' to 'a' instead of comparing them. Although the program compiles and runs successfully, it does not produce the expected output because of the logical error. We can identify this semantic error either by manual code review, or by using static/dynamic code analyzers.

Be sure to understand that pinpointing semantic errors is a complex task that requires a good understanding of the programming language and the logic behind the program's operation. Some semantic errors may only appear under specific conditions during the program's execution and can thus be very hard to anticipate and prevent.

Here is a simple Java method to generate a Fibonacci sequence based on three numbers and determine the highest one. This method receives three integral values as parameters and generates a Fibonacci sequence. This sequence starts with the two smallest values (considering they must be positive or zero), calculates the next Fibonacci number and compares it with the third parameter. 

Also, to account for negative numbers, the method checks if any of the original given parameters are negative. It replaces a negative parameter with its absolute value for the Fibonacci sequence calculation.

Here's a solution:

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(getFibonacciMaximum(-10, 1, 3));    // for example
    }

    public static int getFibonacciMaximum(int num1, int num2, int num3) {
        // Get absolute values of the numbers to deal with negative values
        num1 = Math.abs(num1);
        num2 = Math.abs(num2);
        num3 = Math.abs(num3);

        // Ensure the numbers are in ascending order (num1 <= num2 <= num3)
        if(num1 > num2 || num1 > num3 || num2 > num3) {
            if(num2 > num1 && num2 > num3) {
                // Swap num2 and num3
                int temp = num2;
                num2 = num3;
                num3 = temp;
            }
            if(num1 > num2) {
                // Swap num1 and num2
                int temp = num1;
                num1 = num2;
                num2 = temp;
            }
        }

        int fibonacci = num1 + num2;

        // Check if we have exceeded the third number
        while (fibonacci < num3) {
            num1 = num2;
            num2 = fibonacci;

            fibonacci = num1 + num2;
        }

        // Return the maximum (either the fibonacci reached or the original third number)
        return Math.max(fibonacci, num3);
    }
}
```

This program considers the three input numbers and generates a Fibonacci sequence to find the largest number at the end. Note that this program does not work correctly if the three original numbers are not in ascending order, in accordance with Fibonacci sequence (+1+1=2, 1+2=3, 2+3=5, etc.).

