```java
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class StringCleaner {
    private static final Pattern punctuation = Pattern.compile("\\p{Punct}|\\s+");

    public static String clean(String input) {
        return punctuation.matcher(input).replaceAll("");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(clean("Hello, world! This is a test..."));
    }
}
```

This code uses the `java.util.regex.Pattern` class to compile a regular expression that matches any punctuation character (`\\p{Punct}` covers all unicode punctuation) or any string of one or more whitespace characters (`\\s+`). We then use the `matcher(String input)` method of the `Pattern` instance to get a `Matcher` for the input string, and `replaceAll(String replacement)` will replace every sequence that matches the pattern with the replacement string, in this case, an empty string.

The `main()` method simply tests this with a hardcoded string, but you can modify it to read from a file or take user input, as the requirements of your program demand.

