The JavaScript function `removeFromArray` does not use any built-in functions for removing elements, it uses a simple loop to iterate through the inputArray and checks if the current element is in the elementsToRemove array. If it's not in the elementsToRemove array, it's then pushed into a new array which is returned at the end. 

Here is the JavaScript code of the function: 

```javascript 
function removeFromArray(inputArray, elementsToRemove) {
  // handle null or undefined inputs
  if(!inputArray || !elementsToRemove || !Array.isArray(inputArray) || !Array.isArray(elementsToRemove)) {
    return [];
  }

  let resultArray = [];

  for(let i = 0; i < inputArray.length; i++) {
    let element = inputArray[i];
    if(!elementsToRemove.includes(element)) {
      resultArray.push(element);
    }
  }

  return resultArray;
}
```

