Here is a solution using SQL and anonymous PL/SQL block. Below are the assumptions for this problem:

1. The 'inventory' table has columns 'product_id', 'price', 'expiration_date', and 'perishable_flag' where 'perishable_flag = 1' indicates a perishable item.

2. The 'changes_log' table has columns 'transaction_time', 'product_id', 'old_price', and 'new_price'. 

```sql
DECLARE
   l_old_price inventory.price%TYPE;
   l_new_price inventory.price%TYPE;
BEGIN
   FOR rec IN (SELECT * FROM inventory WHERE perishable_flag = 1 AND expiration_date < SYSDATE)
   LOOP
      l_old_price := rec.price;
      l_new_price := rec.price * 0.85;

      UPDATE inventory SET price = l_new_price WHERE product_id = rec.product_id;
      INSERT INTO changes_log(transaction_time, product_id, old_price, new_price) 
      VALUES(SYSDATE, rec.product_id, l_old_price, l_new_price);
   END LOOP;

   COMMIT;
EXCEPTION
   WHEN OTHERS THEN
      ROLLBACK; 
      RAISE;
END;
```

This PL/SQL block will loop through all perishable items in the inventory with an expiration date in the past. It stores the old price for each item, calculates the new price after applying the discount, updates the price in the inventory, and logs the changes to the 'changes_log' table. If an exception is thrown, it will rollback all changes and re-raise the exception to be handled by the outer block. Please ensure that you have the required permissions to UPDATE and INSERT to these tables.

