Here is a simple Java program to implement binary search:

```java
public class BinarySearch {
    int binarySearch(int arr[], int key) {
        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == key)
                return mid;
            if (arr[mid] < key)
                left = mid + 1;
            else
                right = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String args[]) {
        BinarySearch bs = new BinarySearch();
        int arr[] = {1, 5, 10, 13, 15, 20, 25};
        int key = 20;
        int result = bs.binarySearch(arr, key);
        if (result == -1)
            System.out.println("Element not present in array");
        else
            System.out.println("Element found at index " + result);
    }
}
```

In this program, `binarySearch` function returns the index of `key` in `arr`[] if the `key` is present, else it returns `-1`. It operates by effectively dividing the search space in half at each step, which is why it is named "binary search". In the `main` method, the function `binarySearch` is called on the array `arr` with the `key` value to be found. The result is then printed out. If the element is found, its index is displayed else a message saying the element is not present is displayed.

