Assuming the input is in normal decimal form, we will first check if it's a positive integer. Then, convert it into binary and check the length of the binary representation. If it's in the range of 10 to 99, it means it's a double-digit binary number, since in binary two digits can express 4 different integer values (00, 01, 10, 11). Finally, we will check if it's a palindrome.

Here is the C++ code which accomplishes this:

```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <bitset>
#include <string>

bool isPalindrome(string str) {
    int start = 0, end = str.length() - 1;
    while (start < end) {
        if (str[start] != str[end])
            return false;
        start++;
        end--;
    }
    return true;
}

int main() {
    int num;
    std::cout << "Enter a positive integer number: ";
    std::cin >> num;
    if (num < 0) {
        std::cout << "The number is not a positive integer.\n";
        return 0;
    }

    std::string binary = std::bitset<32>(num).to_string();

    // Remove leading zeros
    binary = binary.substr(binary.find_first_not_of('0'));

    if (! (binary.length() >= 2 && binary.length() <= 32)) {
        std::cout << "The number is not a double-digit binary number.\n";
    } else {
        if (isPalindrome(binary)) {
            std::cout << "The binary of the number is a palindrome.\n";
        } else {
            std::cout << "The binary of the number is not a palindrome.\n";
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This program receives an input number from the user, checks the conditions, and prints the relevant output. The function `isPalindrome` checks whether a given string is a palindrome or not. The `main` function does the major part of taking the number, converting it to binary, and checking the condition. The C++ `bitset` class is used to convert the decimal number to its binary form.

Following is a javascript function to insert divs with unique ids into a parent div. It also contains a check to prevent insertion of divs with duplicate ids. It is assumed that the parentDiv already exists in the DOM. This function also optimizes for page rendering speed by attaching the divs to a document fragment before adding them to the DOM:

```javascript
function insertDivs(parentDivId, numOfDivs) {
    var parentDiv = document.getElementById(parentDivId);

    if (!parentDiv) {
        console.error("Parent Div with Id " + parentDivId + " does not exist.");
        return;
    }

    var fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();
    var idSet = new Set();

    // Checking for duplicate ids among currently existing divs
    var existingDivs = document.getElementsByTagName("div");
    for(let i = 0; i < existingDivs.length; i++) {
        if (existingDivs[i].id) {
            idSet.add(existingDivs[i].id);
        }
    }

    for(let i = 1; i <= numOfDivs; i++) {
        var divId = parentDivId + "-" + i;

        // Check that the div id is unique
        if (idSet.has(divId)) {
            console.error('A div with id ' + divId + ' already exists.')
            return;
        }

        var newDiv = document.createElement('div');
        newDiv.id = divId;
        newDiv.innerText = 'Div ' + i;
        fragment.appendChild(newDiv);

        idSet.add(divId);
    }

    parentDiv.appendChild(fragment);
}
```

This function optimizes page rendering speed by minimizing reflows, as it only touches the DOM once to add all of the new divs. It also prevents the creation of divs with duplicate ids by keeping track of all ids in the idSet and checking each new id against this set. If a duplicate is found, it logs an error and stops execution.

