Here is a JavaScript function that performs binary search on a sorted array, even with duplicate values. This function will return the first occurrence of the target element if it is present, or -1 if it is not:

```javascript
function binarySearch(arr, target) {
    let left = 0;
    let right = arr.length - 1;

    while (left < right) {
        const mid = Math.floor((right + left) / 2);

        if (arr[mid] < target) {
            left = mid + 1;
        } else {
            right = mid;
        }
    }

    if (arr[left] === target) {
        return left;
    }

    return -1;
}
```

In this function, the binary search is done in a while loop. The left and right indices are moved according to the comparison between the mid element (arr[mid]) and the target. If the mid element is less than the target, then the target is in the right half, so the left index is moved to mid + 1. Otherwise, if the mid element is greater or equal to the target, the target may still be in the left half, so the right index is moved to mid. The process is repeated until left and right converge.

After the while loop, the element at the "left" index (which is also the "right" index at this point) is checked. If it's equal to the target, then its index (left) is returned. If not, it means that the target is not in the array, so -1 is returned.

Please note that this function assumes the input array is sorted. If it's not, binary search cannot work properly. If you have an unsorted array, you should sort it first before invoking this function.

