Using CSS is a great way to layer multiple responsive HTML elements and bring a specific one to the front of the page. This can be achieved using the "z-index" property in CSS.

The "z-index" property specifies the stack order of an element, where an element with a greater stack order is always drawn in front of one with a lower stack order. 

For example, if you have two elements, a and b, and you want to bring a to the front, you can give a higher z-index value as compared to b:

```css
#elementA {
   z-index: 2;
}

#elementB {
   z-index: 1;
}
```

To ensure proper visibility already screen adaptation for both small and large resolutions, you can use CSS media queries. A media query is a CSS technique used to apply different styles for different media types/devices.

For illustration, if you want your webpage to adapt to different screen sizes, you can use the following media query:

```css
@media screen and (max-width: 600px) {
    body {
        background-color: lightblue;
    }
}
```

In the example above, when the width of the viewing area is less than 600px, it will apply a lightblue background color. You could use similar logic to adjust fonts, show or hide elements, and so on. 

This approach should provide both layering of elements and responsiveness for different screen sizes.

