Here is a basic design for a REST API using Node.js and Express.js to manipulate US sales information:

First, make sure to import the necessary libraries:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

```
Define a sample data structure representing sales data:

```javascript
let salesData = [
    { id: 1, customerName: "John", product: "Laptop", amount: 500, state: "NY" },
    //...
];
```

Creating a new sales entry:
```javascript
app.post('/sales', (req, res) => {
    let newSale = {
        id: salesData.length + 1,
        customerName: req.body.customerName,
        product: req.body.product,
        amount: req.body.amount,
        state: req.body.state,
    };

    if (!newSale.customerName || !newSale.product || !newSale.amount || !newSale.state) {
        return res.status(400).send('Missing data fields');
    }

    salesData.push(newSale);
    res.status(201).send(newSale);
});
```

Retrieving all sales entries:
```javascript
app.get('/sales', (req, res) => {
    res.send(salesData);
});
```

Retrieving a specific sales entry:
```javascript
app.get('/sales/:id', (req, res) => {
    let sale = salesData.find(item => item.id === parseInt(req.params.id));
    if (!sale) {
        return res.status(404).send('Sales record not found');
    }

    res.send(sale);
});
```

Updating a sales entry:
```javascript
app.put('/sales/:id', (req, res) => {
    let sale = salesData.find(item => item.id === parseInt(req.params.id));
    if (!sale) {
        return res.status(404).send('Sales record not found');
    }

    if (req.body.customerName) {
        sale.customerName = req.body.customerName;
    }

    if (req.body.product) {
        sale.product = req.body.product;
    }

    if (req.body.amount) {
        sale.amount = req.body.amount;
    }

    if (req.body.state) {
        sale.state = req.body.state;
    }

    res.send(sale);
});
```

Start the server:
```javascript
const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(port, () => console.log(`Listening on port ${port}...`));
```

For debugging and improvement, lets consider the PUT method. 

```javascript
app.put('/sales/:id', (req, res, next) => {
    let sale = salesData.find(item => item.id === parseInt(req.params.id));

    if (req.body.customerName) {
        sale.customerName = req.body.customerName;
    }

    if (req.body.product) {
        sale.product = req.body.product;
    }

    if (req.body.amount) {
        sale.amount = req.body.amount;
    }

    if (req.body.state) {
        sale.state = req.body.state;
    }

    res.send(sale);
});
```

In this code, we search the `salesData` array for the item with the provided id. If the item doesn't exist, `sale` will be `undefined`, and when we try to access any property of `sale` (like `sale.customerName`), it will throw a TypeError. 

To avoid this, we need to check if `sale` is `undefined` before trying to access any of its properties. If `sale` is `undefined`, we should send a 404 response to the client. Here is the corrected code:

```javascript
app.put('/sales/:id', (req, res, next) => {
    let sale = salesData.find(item => item.id === parseInt(req.params.id));

    if (!sale) {
        return res.status(404).send('Sales record not found');
    }

    if (req.body.customerName) {
        sale.customerName = req.body.customerName;
    }

    if (req.body.product) {
        sale.product = req.body.product;
    }

    if (req.body.amount) {
        sale.amount = req.body.amount;
    }

    if (req.body.state) {
        sale.state = req.body.state;
    }

    res.send(sale);
});
```

Please note that this is a basic implementation. In a real-world application, sales data would likely be stored in a database. Also, the server would need to handle authentication and authorization.

