Here is a sample Java program that achieves the required tasks:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class StringList {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> stringList = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList("John", "Alice", "Bob"));

        // step 1: Reverse the list
        Collections.reverse(stringList);
        System.out.println("Reversed List: "+ stringList);

        // step 2: Sort the reversed list lexicographically
        Comparator<String> ignoreSpaceComparator = new Comparator<String>() {
            public int compare(String o1, String o2) {
                return o1.replace(" ", "").compareToIgnoreCase(o2.replace(" ", ""));
            }
        };
        Collections.sort(stringList, ignoreSpaceComparator);
        System.out.println("Sorted Reversed List: "+ stringList);
    }
}
```

Time Complexity Analysis:

The time complexity of reversing a list of n elements using `Collections.reverse()` method is O(n).

The time complexity of sorting a list of n elements using `Collections.sort()` method is O(n log n). But as we have used a custom comparator which performs operations (removing spaces and converting to lowercase) on strings and each operation could take up to O(m), where m is the length of the string, the final time complexity would become O(m*n log n).

So, the total time complexity of the solution is O(n + m*n log n) which can be approximated to the maximum value, i.e., O(m*n log n) because m*n log n >= n which is a reasonable complexity for this kind of problem.

