In Java, you can use the Collections.synchronizedList() method which returns a synchronized (thread-safe) list backed by the specified list.

Here is your Java code snippet:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> synchronizedArrayList = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<String>());

        Thread thread1 = new Thread() {
            public void run() {
                for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
                    synchronizedArrayList.add("Thread 1 Added Element " + i);
                    System.out.println("Thread 1 Added Element " + i);
                }
            }
        };

        Thread thread2 = new Thread() {
            public void run() {
                for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
                    synchronizedArrayList.add("Thread 2 Added Element " + i);
                    System.out.println("Thread 2 Added Element " + i);
                }
            }
        };

        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();

        // wait for threads to end
        try {
            thread1.join();
            thread2.join();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Interrupted");
        }

        System.out.println("\nArrayList Contents:");
        // iterator should be used in a synchronized block
        synchronized(synchronizedArrayList) {
            Iterator<String> iterator = synchronizedArrayList.iterator(); 
            while (iterator.hasNext())
                System.out.println(iterator.next());
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we have a synchronized ArrayList which is populated by two threads (`thread1` and `thread2`). Each thread adds 5 elements to the ArrayList. After both threads finish their execution, we print the contents of the ArrayList. The synchronized block is needed when we iterate over the ArrayList to avoid concurrent modification exceptions.

