In JavaScript, to execute a specific instruction within a looping control structure, you often use a "for" loop, a "while" loop, or a "do while" loop. 

Here is an example of how you can do this with a for loop:

```javascript
for (var i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
   console.log(i); // This instruction will be executed in each iteration.
}
```

In this loop, the console.log(i) is the specific instruction executed for each iteration. The initialization expression is "var i = 0", the condition is "i < 10", and the increment expression is "i++". This means the loop will start at 0 and increment by 1 until it is no longer less than 10.

Here is how you can do this with a "while" loop:

```javascript
var i = 0;
while (i < 10) {
   console.log(i); // This instruction will be executed for each iteration.
   i++;
}
```

And this is how you can do it with a "do while" loop:

```javascript
var i = 0;
do {
   console.log(i); // This instruction will be executed for each iteration.
   i++;
} while (i <10);
```

In each of this example, the structure is slightly different, but the specific action (console.log(i)) is repeated until the condition of the loop is no longer true.

