Here is a simple problem-solving for implementing a LRU cache in Java:

Step 1: Understand the problem
A Least Recently Used (LRU) cache is a type of cache in which we remove the least recently used items first. In other words, when the cache is full, and we want to add a new key-value pair to the cache, we remove the least recently used item.

Step 2: Choose the data structures
To implement LRU Cache we will use two data structures: LinkedHashSet and HashMap. LinkedHashSet will store the keys, and HashMap will be used to store key-value pairs.

Step 3: Design the methods
We require three methods for our LRU Cache:
- get(key): It will return the value of the key if it exists, else return -1.
- put(key, value): Insert or update the value of the key.
- removeOldestEntry(): It will remove the oldest/least recently used entry from the cache.

Now, let's implement LRU cache:

    import java.util.HashMap;
    import java.util.LinkedHashSet;

    public class LRUCache {

        private final int capacity;
        private HashMap<Integer, Integer> cache;
        private LinkedHashSet<Integer> keys;

        public LRUCache(int capacity) {
            this.capacity = capacity;
            cache = new HashMap<>();
            keys = new LinkedHashSet<>();
        }

        public int get(int key) {
            if (!cache.containsKey(key))
                return -1;

            keys.remove(key);
            keys.add(key); // add key as the most recently used
            return cache.get(key);
        }

        public void put(int key, int value) {
            if (cache.containsKey(key))
                keys.remove(key);
            else if (keys.size() == capacity)
                removeOldestEntry();

            cache.put(key, value);
            keys.add(key);
        }

        private void removeOldestEntry() {
            int oldestKey = keys.iterator().next();
            keys.remove(oldestKey);
            cache.remove(oldestKey);
        }
    }

Step 4: Validate the solution
