You seem to have misunderstood the purpose of a binary search. Binary search is used to find the position of a given element in a sorted array, not to retrieve an element at a given index. Furthermore, the array must be sorted in ascending or descending order for binary search to work.

If you just want to retrieve an element given its index, you don't need to conduct a binary search at all. You can directly access the element using the index in constant time, meaning the time complexity is O(1). Here is a simple method in Java to do that:

```java
public int getElementAtIndex(int[] array, int index) {
    if (index >= 0 && index < array.length) {
        return array[index];
    } else {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Index out of range");
    }
}```

If you want to conduct a binary search to find the position of a given element in a sorted array, here is an example of this. But, in order to use it, you need to have your array sorted either in ascending or descending order:

```java
public int binarySearch(int[] array, int target) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = array.length - 1;
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (array[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        } else if (array[mid] < target) {
            left = mid + 1;
        } else {
            right = mid - 1;
        }
    }
    return -1;  // return -1 if target not found
}```

Remember, in binary search, the array needs to be sorted because the whole idea of binary search is to exploit the fact that the array is sorted by continually halving the section of the array to search, which leads to the O(log n) time complexity. So, make sure your array is sorted before using this binarySearch method.

