The following method uses formal way of checking email validity. It uses `Pattern` and `Matcher` classes to ensure the email address complies the rules above and does not contain special characters. 

```Java
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;

public class EmailValidator {

    // Email regex string
    private static final String EMAIL_PATTERN = "^[A-Za-z0-9+_.-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+$";

    private static final Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(EMAIL_PATTERN);

    public static boolean isValidEmail(String email) {
        if (email == null) {
            return false;
        }
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(email);
        if (!matcher.matches())
            return false;

        int atIdx = email.indexOf('@');
        int dotIdx = email.lastIndexOf('.');

        if(atIdx < 1 || dotIdx < atIdx+2 || dotIdx+2 >= email.length())
            return false;

        if(email.contains(".."))
            return false;

        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args){
        System.out.println(isValidEmail("test@gmail.com")); // true
        System.out.println(isValidEmail("test..email@gmail.com")); // false
        System.out.println(isValidEmail("test@gm@ail.com")); // false
        System.out.println(isValidEmail("test@g..m")); // false
        System.out.println(isValidEmail("test")); // false
    }
}
```
Calling `isValidEmail` with an email address will return `true` if the email is valid according to the rules and `false` otherwise.

