Here's a Java solution using HashMap to keep track of the numbers and their respective indices encountered so far. When we encounter a new number, we check whether there's a complement number that we've encountered before. If that's the case, the solution is found.

```Java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class Main {

    public static int[] twoSum(int[] numbers, int target) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> numMap = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
            int complement = target - numbers[i];
            if (numMap.containsKey(complement)) {
                return new int[] { numMap.get(complement), i };
            } else {
                numMap.put(numbers[i], i);
            }
        }
        return new int[] {};
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {2, 7, 11, 15};
        int target = 9;
        int[] result = twoSum(numbers, target);
        System.out.println("[" + result[0] + ", " + result[1] + "]");
    }
}
```
This program will output "[0, 1]", which indicates the positions of the numbers whose sum is the target. Note that the function will return an empty array if there is no such pair of numbers. 

Remember that HashMap operation is constant time on average. Hence, the time complexity of this solution is O(n), and the space complexity is also O(n) because we store each number in the HashMap.

