Here's a simple C++ function that uses the built-in sort function from the algorithm library to sort an array in ascending order.

```C++
#include<iostream>
#include<algorithm>
using namespace std;

void sortArray(int arr[], int n){
    sort(arr, arr+n);
}

int main() {
    int unsorted_arr[] = {45, 21, 12, 56, 7, 47, 34};
    int n = sizeof(unsorted_arr)/sizeof(unsorted_arr[0]);

    sortArray(unsorted_arr, n);

    // Printing sorted array
    cout << "Sorted array is: ";
    for (int i=0; i<n; i++)
        cout << unsorted_arr[i] << " ";
    cout << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

The `sort()` function here accepts two arguments, the beginning and the end of the array to sort. In this case, `arr` points to the beginning of the array and `arr+n` points to the end. After calling `sortArray()`, the array is sorted in ascending order.

This "superior data organization" is known as sorting, which rearranges the elements in an array according to a certain order. In this case, the order is ascending numerical value.

