Given the broadness of the problem, let's develop a robust data structure like a "Task Manager" which can uphold a diverse set of task items. We will use a class-based approach in Javascript and showcase complex functionalities like adding tasks, deleting tasks, updating tasks, marking tasks as complete, and even getting all tasks.

```javascript
class Task {
  constructor(id, name, description, isCompleted){
    this.id = id;
    this.name = name; 
    this.description = description;
    this.isCompleted = isCompleted;  
  }
}

class TaskManager {
  constructor() {
    this.tasks = [];
  }

  addTask(task) {
    this.tasks.push(task);
  }

  deleteTask(id) {
    this.tasks = this.tasks.filter(task => task.id !== id);
  }

  updateTask(updatedTask) {
    this.tasks = this.tasks.map(task =>
      task.id === updatedTask.id ? updatedTask : task
    );
  }

  markTaskAsComplete(id) {
    this.tasks = this.tasks.map(task => 
      task.id === id ? {...task, isCompleted: true} : task
    );
  }

  getTasks() {
    return this.tasks;
  }
}

// Using the TaskManager
const taskManager = new TaskManager();

// Creating new tasks
let task1 = new Task(1, "task1", "This is task1 description", false);
let task2 = new Task(2, "task2", "This is task2 description", false);

// Adding tasks
taskManager.addTask(task1);
taskManager.addTask(task2);

// Mark task as complete
taskManager.markTaskAsComplete(1);

// Updating a task
let task1Updated = new Task(1, "task1 updated", "This is updated task1 description", false);
taskManager.updateTask(task1Updated);

// Deleting task
taskManager.deleteTask(2);

// Getting tasks
console.log(taskManager.getTasks());

```
In this code, we have two classes Task and TaskManager. The Task class holds the information of a task, while the TaskManager class holds a list of tasks and has methods for adding, deleting, updating tasks and marking them as complete. This extensive task handling can efficiently meet the requirements of sophisticated systems.

